    "name": "Eddie Vance",
    "role": "Inside man, bank janitor, 80",
    "note": "He took the janitor's job in 1985 as cover for one night and simply never left, becoming the bank's beloved institution. Eddie knows every camera, every guard's grandchild and every vent — and that demolishing the building means demolishing the only life he built out of the worst night of it.",
    "profile": {
     "background": "The Marlowe Street gang's inside man. In 1985 he took a janitor's job at the First Mercantile as cover for a single night's attempt to retrieve what the 1984 job had left in the building; the attempt failed when a vault refit sealed the target behind new steel lining. He stayed a week to watch the work, then a month, then forty years, guarding the wall by sweeping around it and becoming the bank's beloved institution.",
     "personality": "Warm, unhurried and apparently transparent, which forty years of practice have made a perfect professional surface. He knows every camera, every guard's grandchild and every vent, and carries the building's human news the way other men carry keys. He schedules a conspiracy as a retirement planning seminar and files a heist as a fixtures catalogue, correctly, in triplicate.",
     "motivation": "To protect, at once, the two things demolition threatens together: what the wall holds for Dot's promise, and the life inside the building that is the only home the worst night of his life ever produced.",
     "goal": "To keep the crew's work invisible inside the bank's closing routines, deliver the recovery out of the building through legitimate channels he has spent weeks preparing, and leave the Mercantile, when he must leave it, with his name and his catalogue intact.",
     "need": "To stop keeping his head down. Forty years of safety were purchased by never once standing up inside the building that sheltered him; he needs to discover, before it is demolished, that its regard for him was earned by the man, not merely rented by the mop.",
     "flaws": "A waverer whose caution is indistinguishable, even to himself, from the cover that became his life; the bank's beloved institution whose entire respectability rests on an application file that cannot bear inspection, and who has mistaken being overlooked for being safe.",
     "relationships": "Dot, who asks him to betray his home in order to save what it holds. Pete Alvarez, the young guard he watched grow up inside the building, whose duty forces the report that makes Eddie the investigation's focus, the relationship that prices his midpoint choice. Beatrice Kline, the auditor whose proper attention is the first respect his file has ever received. The bank's staff across four decades, the unnamed family his dedication created.",
     "fears": "That he will be exposed in his own building, by people he helped raise, as a forty-year fraud; and, beneath it, that when the building goes he will discover there was never an Eddie outside its corridors to retire into the world.",
     "dilemma": "Whether to take the clean exit the bank offers, early retirement with pension and reputation intact, and let the personnel question die of his absence, or refuse it and stand, for the first time in forty years, where the investigation can see him; and in the crisis, whether to burn his respectability vouching for an alarm he himself triggered.",
     "transformation": "The fullest arc in the film: from a man who never left because leaving was the risk, through the refusal of the clean exit, to the crisis vouching that spends his respectability deliberately, and an ending in which the building's memory is archived under his own name and a new life, the crew's Tuesdays, is waiting outside it.",
     "contribution": "He is the plan's possibility and its midpoint catastrophe in one person: his knowledge builds the job, his file endangers it, his refusal redeems its terms, his legitimately catalogued fixtures provide its exit, and his vouching buys its crisis minutes."
    }
