     "relationships": "Ada Okonkwo, the investigator who asks his own profession's questions back at him and finally confronts him with his ledger. Inspector Rosa Delgado, whose custody paperwork gives Ada the records his serenity cannot argue with. Meridian's board and researchers, the institution that needed his face and that he allowed to need it.",
     "fears": "That his life's respectable work has been scheduling harm in the grammar of probability; that the premium specification for Ada's inclusion exists in a ledger he fronted; and that his cooperation, when it comes, will be read correctly as the moment the evidence became undeniable, not the moment he became brave.",
     "dilemma": "Protect Meridian and its clients, including governments, and let the Reyes rendering complete its work, or surrender the delivery logs and destroy the institution, his career, and his self-portrait in a single signature.",
     "transformation": "From the model's undecided face to its first unscripted witness. He surrenders the delivery logs, accepts the end of Meridian's legitimacy and his own career and the civil and regulatory reckonings to follow, and becomes, in the film's terms, a component who finally steps out of the composition.",
     "contribution": "He embodies the opposition's respectability: not a villain twirling a model, but an institution's conscience declining to audit itself. His buried delivery-effect finding is the mechanism's documentary proof, and his surrender of the logs identifies the client, arms the arrests, and makes the legislative ending possible."
