   {
    "name": "Pearl Delaney",
    "role": "Family matriarch",
    "note": "Eighty-one, sharp as a blade, and Ruth Ann's mother. She has waited 37 years for someone to ask her the right question, and she intends to decide who pays for the answer.",
    "profile": {
     "background": "Eighty-one years old, Ruth Ann's mother, Caleb's mother, widow of Thomas Delaney, and head of the family that owns Blackwater County's bank. She was in the Delaney house on the night in October 1989 when Ruth Ann came home and died there; she made the call for help that was logged and struck through, allowed it to be withdrawn when the men of her house decided on silence, and folded her daughter's church dress into its cleaner's bag herself. For thirty-seven years she has been the case's true archive and the road story's quiet author, steering even her son's grief toward the survivable version.",
     "personality": "Sharp as a blade, courteous, exact and entirely unsentimental in manner; she decides who pays for an answer the way she decides everything, by first establishing what the answer will do. She speaks in completed sentences, wastes none, and her power is never raised — in a county built on who owes whom, she is the creditor. Where Brandt withholds as service, Pearl withholds as power, and the film marks the difference in every scene they separately play with Dana.",
     "motivation": "To control, at last and on terms she judges, the release of the truth she has carried alone — so that it gives Ruth Ann back her name without being spent as revenge on the children of 1989 or as entertainment for strangers.",
     "goal": "To test whether this sheriff will use the truth by a method Pearl can accept, and only then to give Dana the account of that night that no surviving paper holds.",
     "need": "To stop being her family's archive and its gaoler at once: to admit that the silence she built to protect her children has also imprisoned them, that she folded a dress and withdrew a call, and that a mother's protection, maintained for thirty-seven years, became the county's permission to forget.",
     "flaws": "She treats people she loves as pieces whose positions she is entitled to arrange, steering Caleb's memories and his help without his knowledge; she demands to judge how the truth is used while having decided, unilaterally and for decades, that it would not be used at all; her grief is entirely real, and she has made an instrument of it.",
     "relationships": "Ruth Ann, her daughter, the living, funny, ambitious girl whose memory Pearl both preserves exactly and imprisons inside the story she built. Caleb, her son, whom she protected by steering and damaged by the same act, and whose forgiveness she neither requests nor expects on any schedule but his. Thomas Delaney, her late husband, whose decision she ratified and whose share of the blame her account does not spare. Dana Whitlock, the first questioner whose promised method Pearl judges worthy of the answer.",
     "fears": "That the truth, released carelessly, will convict the dead who cannot answer, ruin the son she saved, and leave Ruth Ann remembered as a scandal instead of a girl; and that she will die, as she has lived since 1989, as the only person who knows — taking her daughter's real story into the same ground the county put it in.",
     "dilemma": "Whether a truth withheld to protect children remains protection when the children are grown and imprisoned by it; whether her own acts that night — the withdrawn call, the folded dress, the road story — should be surrendered to the same judgement she arranges for Tillman and Brandt; and who, in the end, pays for an answer she has priced for thirty-seven years, including whether the payer includes herself.",
     "transformation": "From the archive that would not open and the author of the county's survivable story, to the witness who answers on the reservoir shore, implicates her dead husband and herself, releases her son from her steering, and performs the film's last act of custody in reverse: returning the washed, folded dress to the record instead of the water, and accepting that her family's name will now carry what it did.",
     "contribution": "She is the central conflict's author, archive and final gate: she built the forgetting, tested the investigation through her son, holds the only complete account of the night, and sets the moral terms — method before outcome — on which the film's resolution is permitted to happen."
    }
   }
