   {
    "name": "Claire Voss",
    "role": "Sponsor's representative on board",
    "note": "A mining executive who came to supervise and finds herself the human face of the surface world's intentions. Her orders and her conscience diverge at exactly the depth where radio stops working.",
    "profile": {
     "background": "Forty-eight, a mining executive representing the seabed-mining consortium aboard. She began her career as a survey geologist before moving into the consortium's executive ranks, which is why she can read Duarte's raw sonar, argue a channel joint from close imagery, and draft, in her own cabin, the claim filing her employers expect. She came aboard to supervise a survey, protect the contract and report, carrying a file that describes Duarte as a contested lead.",
     "personality": "Composed, exact and unsentimental; she asks for raw feeds rather than summaries, prices every option including her own, and never pretends an order is anything other than what it is. Her tell is tempo: as her conscience diverges from her instructions, her requests remain identical in wording and grow steadily slower in delivery.",
     "motivation": "At first, professional completion: a clean survey, a defensible filing, a contract honoured. Beneath it, an older geologist's motive she has spent an executive career not examining — she once chose the filing system over the field, and the canyon prices that choice.",
     "goal": "To deliver the consortium its survey: coordinates fixed, samples logged, and a claim filing ready before the expedition withdraws ahead of the storm.",
     "need": "To admit, while it can still change an outcome, that filing is an act with a place at the other end of it — and to let the geologist she was outvote, once, the executive she became.",
     "flaws": "She is complicit by competence: nobody aboard could file the canyon away more skilfully, and her draft claim is excellent. She also begins by using Duarte's scandal as a management instrument — demanding independent verification she knows will be heard as doubt — and her late slowness, however deliberate, is still a form of not-quite-refusing that lets the consortium's order arrive with her name on the transmission.",
     "relationships": "Sponsor's counterpart and professional mirror to Duarte — two women who both began in survey work, divided by what each did when a filing system improved a version of the truth; respectful opponent to Adeyemi, whose corrections she accepts on evidence and resents on schedule; employer-side authority over Webb's contract, if not over his hull, and the recipient of the consortium order she must finally decide whether to execute or falsify.",
     "fears": "That she will file the claim competently and spend the rest of her life as the geologist who recognised coursed, repaired stone at four kilometres and described it, for money, as an empty site; and, practically, that her falsification will be discovered — as she knows it must eventually be — and read as corruption rather than conscience.",
     "dilemma": "Whether to execute a lawful order she now believes describes the canyon falsely; whether slowing the survey without refusing it is honesty or cowardice; and, in the crisis, whether to delete the coordinate fix and file the survey as inconclusive, knowing backups and telemetry make the protection temporary and the personal cost near-certain.",
     "transformation": "From supervisor to witness to accomplice in protection: she watches the raw feed, does her own geology, slows her own survey, and finally files falsely and deletes the fix — not as a permanent victory, which she is too clear-eyed to claim, but as time bought for a world that cannot yet speak in the rooms where its fate is filed.",
     "contribution": "She is the surface world embodied in one cabin: her contract, her verification demands and her draft claim create the extraction pressure, her conversion supplies the ending's second climax, and her knowledge of backups and telemetry is what keeps the ending honest about its own fragility."
    }
   }
